AI is changing how people find answers: generic reference and how‑to content is increasingly satisfied inside assistants, not on the open web.

For sites like mine, this hits encyclopedia‑style backgrounders, Q&A‑ish posts, and “what is X” primers the hardest. Readers still come for analysis, timeliness, or lived experience. But for surface‑level “context,” assistants do the job well enough that the original source is often skipped.

> #### tl;dr
>
> - AI commoditizes surface‑level reference; traffic shifts away from source sites.
> - Blocking crawlers won’t fix distribution or prove you’re in the conversation.
> - Win with human‑centric value: interactive, personal, timely, and experiential content.
> - Measure differently; expect opaque referrals and uneven attribution.
> - A hopeful path: privacy‑respecting human verification in browsers → healthy comments, lightweight payments, and community.

Here’s the reality I see in my work: classic reference material—definitions, quick how‑tos, Stack‑Overflow‑style snippets—gets satisfied inside AI tools. People still seek out primary sources for breaking news, official documentation, regulatory text, or papers where provenance matters. They also show up for opinionated takes and personal narratives, where the author’s voice is the point.

In other words, this isn’t about someone “eating your lunch” in the future. It’s already eaten for certain content types. The job changes from “be the best answer everywhere” to “be the best experience where a visit is obviously better.”

Assistants collapse many open tabs into one answer box. That especially impacts:

- Everyday definitions and encyclopedia‑style backgrounders
- Commodity how‑tos and setup checklists
- Aggregated “best practices” and quick technical guidance

Meanwhile, readers still prefer to visit sources for:

- Timely reporting, original analysis, or opinion
- Deep, consequential reference where provenance matters (docs, specs, research)
- Personal experience and narrative from a trusted author

Think of it like retail. Big‑box convenience drew shoppers away from small specialty stores. But here, the “big box” is built from the small shop’s inventory. The synthesis helps the reader, but it doesn’t return attention, credit, or revenue to the maker by default.

If you publish reference or “context” material, you’re not about to get disrupted—your lunch is already being eaten. Search experiences increasingly satisfy the task without a click. Studies on AI Overviews and zero‑click behavior broadly show suppressed click‑through rates for commodity intents; some publishers have reported double‑digit declines on affected queries as assistants answer in‑place.

### The small shop vs the big box

The big box wins on convenience—one stop, all the basics. The difference now: the “inventory” being consolidated is your writing. It’s adapted, summarized, and combined with others’ work. Sometimes you’re cited; often you’re not. Even when you are, the click‑through is inconsistent and hard to measure.

For creators who don’t depend on ads, this is still maddening because relevance becomes invisible. Are you in the conversation? You might be, but the trail is faint.

## Why “just block the bots” isn’t a strategy

You can attempt to opt out of training or scraping. Even if enforcement improves, you still face:

- Fragmented policies across models and regions
- No reliable way to prove your work influenced an answer
- Lost distribution: fewer people discover you in the first place

The uncomfortable reality: some content will be consumed in assistants no matter what. The question is how to remain relevant and measurable.

For example, you can tell specific crawlers not to index your site for training or retrieval and that might keep your pages out of one model’s diet, but it won’t rebuild distribution, and it won’t stop synthesis from other places that learned similar facts elsewhere. It also doesn’t solve for assistants that extract answers from the broader web graph or cite “nearby” resources.

Could regulation force contribution back to sources? Maybe in places, sometimes. But any legal framework is likely to be fragmented by jurisdiction, model type, and enforcement reality. Even if compensation shows up, it won’t solve distribution, and it’s unlikely to reach smaller blogs consistently.

## Where to invest: value only your site can deliver

Prioritize experiences that are hard to reproduce in an answer box:

- Interactive tools and quizzes that adapt to the reader
- Walkthroughs with live code, data, or media the reader manipulates
- Opinionated guidance tied to your track record and taste
- Behind‑the‑scenes notes: constraints, tradeoffs, and “why,” not just “how”
- Timely series and updates where recency and accountability matter

Also, tighten the basics so visiting the site is undeniably better:

- Fast, clean pages with great typography and zero friction
- Clear IA so the “next question” is one click away
- Descriptive links, accessible images, and tidy Markdown

If the “big box” sells homogenized answers, your “small shop” wins by being unmistakably human: taste, context, and craft. Readers visit when the experience is uniquely yours—because you show the why, not just the what.

### Formats that travel poorly to assistants (that’s good for you)

- Explainers with a stance: you’re not just accurate; you’re useful and opinionated
- Guided choices and tradeoff checklists tailored to a role or constraint
- Interactive demos, data explorers, and calculators
- Training modules with progress, certification, or feedback
- Case studies with artifacts readers can inspect and reuse

### Double down on the basics

When a reader does click through, the on‑site experience has to be delightful:

- Pages that feel instant; typography that’s pleasant; zero cookie walls
- Clear “next step” IA and related reading that builds momentum
- Minimal UI friction for subscribing, saving, or sharing
- Clean Markdown, descriptive links, alt text with meaningful captions

## The measurement problem (and bias risk)

Even when an assistant cites you, referral tracking is inconsistent. Sometimes you’ll see a link; often you won’t. Assistants and in‑SERP summaries don’t always pass referral data, so visits land in “direct” or “unknown.” That obscures whether you’re part of the conversation at all.

Practical ways to get a clearer signal:

- Watch for branded queries rising while non‑brand falls. That pattern often accompanies more in‑place answering.
- Track “assisted discovery” proxies: newsletter signups, repeat visitors, and time‑to‑return.
- Use clearly labeled canonical links, schema, and stable anchors so citations have something durable to point to.

There’s also governance risk. Curators (human and algorithmic) can exclude entire classes of sources. There is no guarantee of neutrality—and no recourse if your work is elided from the synthesis. That’s not only a disadvantage; it’s a distribution risk.

Assume partial opacity. Ship work you’re proud of anyway, and bias your mix toward formats where direct visitation is the obvious win.

Monetization follows the same pattern. When an answer is consumed in‑place, you can’t wrap it in your narrative, your newsletter CTAs, or your on‑page monetization. You’ll see fewer ad impressions and fewer moments to earn a relationship.

### What to track when referrals disappear

- Cohort behavior: do new readers return within 7–14 days?
- Reading depth: are people hitting 2–3 pages per session after their first visit?
- “Save” moments: email signups, RSS, “copy link,” and “add to notes” clicks
- Mentions in public places (GitHub issues, newsletters) when assistants cite you

## A hopeful path: human‑verified, privacy‑respecting participation

The web got less fun when bots overran forums and comments. If browsers shipped an anonymous, privacy‑preserving human verification primitive, we could revive:

- Low‑friction, high‑quality comments and discussions
- Lightweight tips and small subscriptions without heavy identity
- Community presence that rewards showing up as a person

Done right, that’s better than chasing impressions: it rebuilds the social fabric around sites.

We already see the ingredients: privacy‑preserving tokens to attest “a real device, a real user” without revealing identity; evolving standards from the web and privacy communities; platform moves to reduce captchas and automated abuse. If this lands in browsers, we can make comment forms pleasant again, bring back lightweight social presence, and make tip jars and tiny subscriptions practical—no surveillance required.

Imagine if browsers shipped a built‑in, privacy‑respecting “human presence” signal that any site could verify without tracking. Comments become less spammy without forcing logins into ad‑tech silos. “Thanks” buttons can move a few cents without a full billing relationship. Small communities can form around posts again. The web gets a little more like the earlier years—personal, conversational, and fun.

## What I’m focusing on

- More posts where my perspective (not just instructions) matters
- Interactive pieces that reward being on‑site
- Cleaner IA and faster pages so reading is pleasant
- Light community features if verification primitives improve

Practically, that means:

- Turning some reference drafts into “explainers with a stance,” linking out to short how‑tos
- Shipping small interactive notebooks, checklists, or calculators alongside posts
- Polishing internal search and related‑reading trails so the “next step” is obvious
- Writing fewer, better context posts—and accepting that many will be read via assistants

## Playbooks you can borrow

If you’re maintaining a docs site, blog, or knowledge base and feeling the traffic shift:

1) Separate “reference” from “experience”

- Keep concise, factual reference pages fast and linkable. Expect them to be quoted more than visited.
- Put your energy into companion pieces: “why we chose X,” “how to decide Y,” “what broke and what we learned.”

2) Design for the first on‑site minute

- Start with value (not a preamble). Put the code, diagram, or takeaway first.
- Offer one obvious next step: demo, checklist, or “try it” interaction.

3) Make the site a better assistant

- Show contradictions: compare approaches side‑by‑side with context
- Offer “it depends” logic: short quizzes that route to relevant guidance
- Link provenance obsessively so readers can audit claims

4) Treat measurement as directional

- Use analytics to spot patterns (brand up, non‑brand down) rather than absolutist truths
- Watch retention, not just sessions; improve the reasons to return

5) Invite participation, lightly

- Comments or notes if you can keep them civil; otherwise, links to a place you’ll actually respond
- Tiny tips or low‑friction support for readers who found it useful

## Closing

Assistants are here and useful. The opportunity is to double down on the parts of the web we actually like: human voices, interactive learning, and places that feel good to visit. If you have ideas—or want to try an interactive format together—I’d love to hear them.

